WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
Personal dietary choices are an
extraordinarily important cause - in fact,

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.760
they're the single biggest cause of lost
years of healthy life in the developed

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.389
world. And they're a really strong
competitor for the single biggest cause

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:16.980
in the developing world too. Unhealthy dietary choices are

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.720
five times as important as physical
inactivity as a driver of premature

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.349
death and disease. And so for that reason,
and they're the major thing behind this

00:00:27.349 --> 00:00:31.830
skyrocketing rate of global obesity. This
is in the US but the rate, the global

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:37.170
rate, the curve looks pretty similar.
And for this reason cost-effective

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.430
scaleable interventions to get people to
change their health, their dietary

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.809
choices are a very important scientific
priority. They have been for about 30

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:50.700
years. And by the way, the changes
don't have to be big to be very

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.510
important. So for example, there was an
estimate published in The Lancet in 2011

00:00:54.510 --> 00:01:01.050
that as of 2011 as little as a 20
calorie per person reduction on average

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.000
across the US and UK would have gotten
those countries to 1990s levels of

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.229
obesity within three years. It's a tiny
tiny change. But it's got to be on a

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:16.920
massive scale, and it's got to be
sustained. And so because it's been this

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.670
scientific priority, there have been lots
of people who've tried to develop

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.210
interventions to get people to change
their dietary habits. They tend to focus

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:30.509
on the classroom partly because you want
large scale change and schools are one

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:35.460
of the rare opportunities to get access
to basically the entire population at a

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:42.899
at a given age. And so one thing that
these interventions basically all have

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:46.259
in common is that they involve some
combination of health and nutrition

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:48.899
education. So what's good for you, what's
bad for you, how do you read the

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:52.110
nutrition labels, and what's called
skills training. And that usually

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:57.270
involves a combination of learning how
to set realistic goals, analyzing

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.829
barriers to behavior change, that sort of
thing. The other big thing they all have

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:05.700
in common is that they've failed, right?
So people have been trying to do this

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.220
for like 30 years. I'm not aware of a
single intervention that keeps affecting

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.299
people's behavior after you stop
intervening.

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:19.769
And I think the major reason for this is
that they all share either implicitly or

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:24.629
explicitly an underlying assumption. And
that is that long term self-interest, the

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:27.930
desire to be healthy in the distant
future, is an effective motivator for

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:32.420
teenagers. And I mean I don't need
citations, but I have them, but like,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:39.299
obviously that's not the case. And so our
approach has been to, rather than trying

00:02:39.299 --> 00:02:43.859
to get adolescents to care about things
they don't care about, reframe the

00:02:43.859 --> 00:02:46.829
meaning of healthy eating so that it's
consistent with the things they do care

00:02:46.829 --> 00:02:52.829
about. We focus on three major values in
particular. One is the sort of

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:57.420
well-known teen drive to rebel against
adult authority. Adolescents hate doing

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.629
what adults tell them to do, they'll
often do the opposite just to reassert

00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:06.870
their autonomy. Less widely-appreciated
is adolescence is a period where there's

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:11.900
a real increase in concern for fairness
and justice and beyond the self aims.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.810
Just to give you a couple of quick
examples: this is the time in life when

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.900
people often first decide to become
vegetarians for moral reasons or

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.069
anti-globalization activists. They have a
sense of themselves as agents in the

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:26.489
world for the first time and they want
to make the world a better place. And

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:31.699
unlike most adults they're not yet jaded
about their prospects for doing that.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.609
Yeah, this collection of optimistic
people was the wrong place to make that

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:38.150
cynical joke.

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:45.420
Okay and then the third
big value is gonna be familiar again,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.420
that's the drive for peer approval and
status. It's certainly not unique to

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.609
adolescents but it actually is very
strongly heightened in adolescents. And a

00:03:53.609 --> 00:03:57.420
big reason for that is that the
central task, developmental task of

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.200
adolescence, is figuring out how to be
a social agent. So if you're going to do

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.930
that, you need to be very attentive to
what's being approved of and what's not.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:11.370
And so what we did to try to appeal to
these three values was essentially, it

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:16.109
took a two-fold approach. The first is we
framed the entire intervention as an

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:20.340
expose of manipulative food industry
marketing practices aimed at tricking

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.650
people into eating more junk food than
they would naturally be inclined to eat.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:30.990
And here I'm talking about things like
using deceptive labeling to conceal the

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.169
sugar content in food or spending
unbelievable sums of money on

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:37.710
laboratories that are specifically
designed to figure out how you can

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.270
formulate foods to make them as
addictive as possible. The second thing

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.199
we did was emphasize the social justice
consequences of this food marketing. And

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:50.760
here I'm talking in particular about
disproportionately targeting very young

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.780
children, poor people and minorities with
marketing of the unhealthiest foods

00:04:54.780 --> 00:05:01.349
because they're easy targets. And so our
goal here was to frame healthy eating as

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:06.360
a way to stick it to the man, to rebel
against a controlling adult authority

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.220
figure who's trying to control us, and as
a way to stand up for people who are

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.930
less able to defend themselves - both of
which were sort of deliberately

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:20.370
crafted to appeal to the to this sort of
adolescent set of values and

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:26.639
sensibilities. And so essentially what we
were aiming to do here was to

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:31.470
replace what we imagined adolescents'
sort of default construal, their default

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:35.280
understanding of what a healthy eater is.
Something like, "Healthy eaters are lame

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.320
nerds who do what adults tell them to do"
with an alternative construal: "Healthy

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.420
eaters are independent minded people who
are fighting to make the world a fairer

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:44.960
place."

